    "title": "Rock Island to take over operation of rail line in Watsonville, Calif. - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By David Lassen ,\n6 hours ago Rock Island Rail will become the contract operator of 2.6 miles of track in the Watsonville, Calif., area. Rock Island via STB WASHINGTON — Shortline company Chicago Rock Island & Pacific will take over as contract operator of 2.6 miles of trackage in California’s Santa Cruz and Monterey counties, according to a notice of exemption filed with the Surface Transportation Board.\nRock Island, based in Gulfport, Miss., will operate on trackage in the Watsonville area owned by the Santa Cruz County Regional Transportation Commission, which plans to eventually develop a 22-mile passenger rail service between Santa Cruz and Pajaro, Calif. [See “Santa Cruz, Calif., rail project …,” Trains.com, June 8, 2025.]\nRock Island will replace Progressive Rail as the operator, a move expected to take place by July 21, according to the STB filing . The SCCRTC and Progressive reached an agreement to end their contract in May, according to a paywalled Santa Cruz Sentinel article . The transportation commission had sought to terminate its agreement in order to build a trail on, rather than next to, the rail right-of-way. While the SCCRTC owns more than 30 miles of right-of-way between Watsonville Junction and Davenport, Calif., Rock Island will operate only on the easternmost 2.6 miles. The route has three customers, according to the filing.\nRock Island operates railroads in Mississippi and Kansas. More information is available at its website.\n— To report news or errors, contact trainsnewswire@firecrown.com .\n2",
    "summary": "1 hours ago Rock Island Rail will become the contract operator of 2.6 miles of track in the Watsonville, Calif., area. Rock Island via STB WASHINGTON — Shortline company Chicago Rock Island & Pacific will take over as contract operator of 2.6 miles of trackage in California’s Santa Cruz and Monterey counties, according to a notice of exemption filed with the Surface Transportation Board. Rock Island will replace Progressive Rail as the operator, a move expected to take place by July 21, according to the STB filing .",
